# Line-ending and byte-identity policy for the reproducible artifact.
#
# `docs/research/thesis-evidence-manifest.json` pins evidence receipts by raw
# SHA-256 over their committed bytes, and `scripts/verify-thesis-evidence.js`
# recomputes each digest from the working tree. Those digests only match when the
# checkout bytes equal the committed blob bytes.
#
# Git's `core.autocrlf=true` — the default written by the Git for Windows
# installer, and the configuration present in the reference development
# environment — rewrites LF to CRLF in the working tree. That changed the bytes
# and made `npm run reproduce:thesis-core` fail on Windows while passing on Linux
# CI. Declaring the policy inside the repository removes the dependence on the
# reproducer's local Git configuration.
#
# `text eol=lf` is what makes the checkout reproducible: `eol=lf` is enforced at
# checkout time and overrides `core.autocrlf`. A plain `-text` marker only
# disables normalisation and still leaves `core.autocrlf=true` free to convert
# the working tree, which is the failure this file exists to prevent.
#
# Text files are therefore stored and materialised with LF on every platform, so
# all operating systems get identical bytes.
* text eol=lf

# Binary assets keep their exact committed bytes. These markers are redundant for
# most of the listed types (Git already treats them as binary) but they state the
# intent explicitly and protect any file whose type detection is ambiguous.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.jar  binary
*.apk  binary
*.aab  binary
*.zip  binary
*.so   binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks  binary
